  ok   the stale-base note prints the recorded and live base SHAs
  ok   a recorded base oid matching the live tip resolves unchanged
  ok   an invalid live-tip lookup result is refused
  ok   a stale recorded base with a moved dev passes the fetch equality check against the resolved live tip
  ok   a head moved between metadata lookup and fetch is refused naming the head
  ok   dev moving between the invocation-time resolve and the fetch is refused naming both base SHAs
  ok   the event carries the recorded base oid, so the local replica classifies the same file set hosted does
  ok   and that is not the validation base, so the payload cannot be mistaken for the commit the trust checks read
  ok   the normal validation path prints the resolved base and enters its Docker boundary
  ok   the normal validation path leases the selected workflow's act job volumes
  ok   dry-run prints the job bound and the bounded container options as one quoted argv word
  ok   an isolated slot's PR run enters the slot with its own endpoint, creates and removes its boundary inside it, and prints act under that slot's namespace and CPUs
  ok   a slot-0 dry run of the shipping rtl-full prints its 4 concurrent containers' own ranges after staging on 0-3, and an act command created on the staging range
  ok   a slot-3 dry run of the shipping rtl-full prints its 4 concurrent containers' own ranges after staging on 60-63, and an act command created on the staging range
  ok   a two-workflow run leases both, and starts the CPU-range monitor, gates, spawns, stops the monitor, cleans containers, then reconciles volumes, once per workflow in that order
  ok   each workflow's CPU-range monitor is handed the run's derived job ranges
  ok   volumes observed on a workflow's containers reach that workflow's reconciliation
  ok   a job volume act left after a workflow refuses the run after removal
  ok   a leftover container refuses the run only after that workflow's volumes were reconciled
  ok   a boundary that does not lease the selected workflow refuses before act
  ok   with a container left on the staging range and act exiting 0, each workflow prints its CPU-range summary and one warning per problem, and the verdict follows act's exit alone
  ok   with a container left on the staging range and act exiting 3, each workflow prints its CPU-range summary and one warning per problem, and the verdict follows act's exit alone
  ok   the action cache is populated exactly once per run, before the Docker boundary exists and before the first act launch
  ok   two workflows sharing two actions produce one clone each, in sorted order
  ok   the clones in the real sequence carry the run's own credential-free git environment
  ok   a failing clone refuses the run naming the action, before any boundary or act launch
  ok   a trusted worktree at the live tip is accepted while the recorded base oid is stale
  ok   the runner's own bytes - that file, in the trusted root, at the validation base and not the recorded oid - are what is verified
  ok   a trusted worktree at the stale recorded base is refused naming the loaded head and the live tip
  ok   a dirty trusted worktree is refused for being dirty, before the base comparison and before any bytes are read
  ok   a trusted worktree carrying replacement refs is refused for those, before the base comparison and before any bytes are read
  ok   the fixture's validated base ref is the constant the fetch targets, so the resolve and the fetch name one ref
  ok   production constructs the validated run exactly once, in the audited resolve, so no second construction can bind the recorded oid as the validation base
  ok   the trusted-runner check defaults to reading the real checkout state and verifying real bytes
  ok   the resolve defaults to the real remote lookup and a flushed note
  ok   a validated run cannot be built on anything but a full commit SHA
  ok   the resolve re-checks the repository name it interpolates, rather than inheriting the check from main's ordering
  ok   and re-checks the base ref it interpolates, for the same reason
  ok   a well-formed ls-remote reply yields the tip
  ok   an ls-remote reply naming no ref is refused
  ok   an ls-remote reply naming another ref is refused
  ok   a ref merely ending in the wanted name is ignored, so one pushed branch or tag cannot refuse every run of the gate
  ok   an ls-remote reply carrying the wanted ref twice is refused
  ok   an ls-remote reply line carrying no ref at all is refused
  ok   all selects each workflow once in stable order
  ok   explicit workflow selection de-duplicates
  ok   all plus another workflow is refused
  ok   cross-repository candidate is refused
  ok   non-open pull request is refused
  ok   non-dev pull request is refused
  ok   malformed GitHub metadata is refused
  ok   act command selects the real strict exhaustive workflow
  ok   candidate checkout is copied from immutable materialization
  ok   job containers receive no Docker socket or host network
  ok   artifact and cache servers bind only to the bridge gateway
  ok   job container options are one argv word: the ownership label, the CPU set, then the memory and swap limits
  ok   act starts at most 1 jobs of a stage at once, stated exactly once
  ok   only an explicitly empty GitHub token reaches candidate jobs
  ok   every ambient act input is replaced by an explicit empty file
  ok   artifact uploads receive a real isolated listener port
  ok   act reads the pre-populated per-head action cache offline, exactly once
  ok   job containers get a cpuset of 0-<n-1> for n = min(host CPUs, 4)
  ok   the CPU bound leaves the unpredictable ownership label in the same container options word
  ok   slot 0 stages on 0-3 and gives four concurrent containers 4-7, 8-11, 12-15, 16-19; one container gets 4-7 alone
  ok   slot 3 stages on 60-63 and gives four concurrent containers 64-67 to 76-79
  ok   the run prints the staging range and every job range as --cpuset-cpus words
  ok   a replay deriving 0 concurrent containers is refused: the slot has 4 ranges
  ok   a replay deriving 5 concurrent containers is refused: the slot has 4 ranges
  ok   slot 1 with four concurrent containers needs CPU 39: a 39-CPU host refuses it, a 40-CPU host gives it exactly 24-27 to 36-39
  ok   slot 0 refuses a host without the ranges a run derives, as every slot does: four containers need CPU 19 (19 CPUs refuse, 20 and 21 give 4-7 to 16-19), one container needs CPU 7 (7 refuse, 8 gives 4-7), and hosts below four CPUs refuse both
  ok   every slot 0-63 running 4 job containers at once: 320 ranges, each 4 CPUs wide, no CPU shared within or across slots
  ok   the overlap finder names the shared CPU of a planted pair and passes adjacent ranges
  ok   planted overlap across slots: the pre-#730 spacing of one range per slot puts slot 0's job ranges on slot 1's CPUs, and the proof fails
  ok   planted overlap within a slot: every leg on the slot's one range, as before #730, fails the proof, leg against leg
  ok   a staging range planted on the first job range is refused before any container moves
  ok   four concurrent containers move to four different ranges, lowest first, each seen there by docker inspect
  ok   a container that died keeps its range until it is removed: after create, update, start and die of A, then create, update and start of B, A starts again on its own range and never shares one with B
  ok   a destroy frees the range for the next container
  ok   with every range held, docker inspect frees the range of a container removed without its event, keeps a stopped one's, and leaves a fifth live container on the staging range, reported
  ok   a container gone before its move frees its range and is counted; a foreign owner, an unexpected action or ID, and an unreadable line are each a problem, never a move; a repeated create moves once; a start whose create was never read is moved
  ok   a move the daemon applied before its CLI timed out keeps its range: docker inspect shows it there, the next container gets another, and the warning says both
  ok   a timed-out move still on the staging range keeps its range reserved while another container is created, so its late completion lands on a range no one else holds
  ok   a move and a read that both time out keep the range reserved, never seen there
  ok   a move the daemon refused keeps its range reserved and is reported
  ok   the monitor streams container events from a second before act, filtered to this boundary's owner label and the five actions it reads, in a session of its own
  ok   after its body the monitor walks the stream's group down the containment ladder once, joins its reader with every line applied, and closes the pipe
  ok   a body that raises still has the stream contained before the exception reaches the Docker boundary
  ok   a stream whose poll fails is still contained once, its reader joined and its pipe closed, and the failed poll is a problem, not a verdict
  ok   an event stream that ended before act is reported as a problem, not a verdict
  ok   a run without job ranges starts no monitor and moves nothing
  ok   a reader still moving a container when the body returns is joined, its move finished and the stream ended by containment, before the monitor lets the Docker boundary unwind
  ok   a containment whose leader reap fails still has the busy reader joined and the pipe closed before the monitor exits, and with no earlier failure the reap failure is what it raises
  ok   a body that raises and a reap that fails together still join the busy reader first; the body's failure is what the monitor raises, and the reap failure is printed beside it
  ok   a body ended by a termination signal still joins the busy reader before the signal reaches the Docker boundary, and the runner exits 143 after cleanup
  ok   a SIGINT that ends the body, then a reap that fails, still joins the busy reader first; the runner exits 130 with its interrupt diagnostic, and the reap failure is printed beside it
  ok   a SIGTERM that ends the body, then a reap that fails, still joins the busy reader first; the runner exits 143 with its interrupt diagnostic, and the reap failure is printed beside it
  ok   a SIGHUP that ends the body, then a reap that fails, still joins the busy reader first; the runner exits 129 with its interrupt diagnostic, and the reap failure is printed beside it
  ok   a stream that cannot start is a Refusal with nothing to contain; a reader that cannot start still has the started stream contained and its pipe closed
  ok   a reader join that fails is retried while the reader still runs after the pause, and the join failure is raised only once the reader has ended, with the pipe closed
  ok   with no failure already propagating, a reap failure and then a join failure raise the first, the reap's, once the reader has ended, and print the join failure beside it
  ok   with an interrupt already propagating, the same reap and join failures leave it to propagate once the reader has ended, and both are printed beside it, in order
  ok   a reader still running when its bounded wait (patched to 0.05 s) expires is held for, not abandoned: the hold is printed and recorded, and the join returns only once the reader has ended
